  {
    "date": "2021-07-04",
    "term": "袖",
    "kana": "そで",
    "meaning": "sleeve",
    "level": "N3",
    "pos": ["noun (common) (futsuumeishi)"],
    "priority": ["ichi1", "news2", "nf25"],
    "stratum": "wago",
    "processes": ["compound", "derivation", "rendaku", "sound-change"],
    "headline": "Sode, “sleeve”, appears to be 衣 (so, “robe; clothing”) plus 手 (te, “hand; arm”), first attested in the Kojiki of 712.",
    "morphemes": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "quote": "⟨so₁ te⟩ → /so te/ → /sode/"
      },
      {
        "quote": "From Old Japanese. First attested in the Kojiki of 712 with the phonetic spelling 蘇弖 (⟨so₁ te⟩). The te changes to de as an instance of rendaku (連濁)."
      },
      {
        "quote": "Japanese references that include an etymology note that this appears to be a compound of 衣 (so, “robe; clothing”) + 手 (te, “hand; arm”). However, the initial so- is clearly spelled with the so-called 甲類 (kōrui, “\"A\" type”) vowel ⟨so₁⟩ in the oldest texts, whereas 衣 when read as so was pronounced with the so-called 乙類 (otsurui, “\"B\" type”) vowel as ⟨so₂⟩. These were treated as separate vowels in Old Japanese, so this proposed compound cannot be the actual derivation."
      }
    ],
    "examples": [
      {
        "id": 80948,
        "ja": "無い袖は振れぬ。",
        "en": "You can't spend what you don't have.",
        "enId": 322767,
        "form": "袖",
        "furigana": [
          ["無", "な"],
